<BaseLayout title="Start Here" description="New to Chinese tales of the strange? Three stories and five concepts will open the whole universe.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <p class="kicker">Start Here</p>
      <h1>New to This Universe?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        You do not need a degree in Sinology. You need three stories, five concepts, and about half an
        hour. Here is the door.
      </p>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:36px;">
        <h2>First: the five-minute map</h2>
        <p>
          The cosmos has <strong>three floors</strong>: a heavenly bureaucracy above, the mortal world in
          the middle where anything can change, and an underworld below that processes the dead like a
          civil service. Everything — gods, ghosts, fox spirits, woken trees — runs on
          <strong> essence</strong>, is policed by <strong>heaven's examinations</strong>, and is entered
          in <strong>karma's ledger</strong>. That is genuinely most of it. If you want the full
          orientation first, take five minutes for the
          <a href="/guides/beginners-guide">Beginner's Guide</a>; if you would rather learn by reading,
          start below.
        </p>

        <h2>Story one: laugh first</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/stories/song-dingbo"><strong>Song Dingbo Catches a Ghost</strong></a> — four hundred
          words, Eastern Jin dynasty. A man meets a ghost on a night road, claims to be one himself,
          learns its single weakness, and sells it at market as a goat. It is the funniest ghost story in
          the tradition, and secretly a manual: ghosts run on rules, and fear is their only real weapon.
        </p>

        <h2>Story two: the hero</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/stories/li-ji"><strong>Li Ji Slays the Serpent</strong></a> — the first great
          monster-slaying in Chinese fiction, won not by a knight or a god but by a thirteen-year-old
          girl with a sword, a hound, and honeyed rice-balls. Watch how it is won: by
          <em>preparation</em>, not magic. That is the tradition in one gesture.
        </p>

        <h2>Story three: the full power</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/stories/nie-xiaoqian"><strong>Nie Xiaoqian</strong></a> — Pu Songling's most famous
          tale. A ruined temple, a ghost forced to prey on travellers, the demon that owns her, a
          swordsman whose blade moves by itself, and a redemption nobody in the genre had quite managed
          before. Everything the tradition can do, in one story.
        </p>

        <h2>Then go deeper</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The <a href="/worldview">Worldview</a> — the encyclopedia of the universe: realms, beings, figures, rules.</li>
          <li>The <a href="/worldview/timeline">Timeline</a> — how the universe grew, era by era.</li>
          <li>The <a href="/features">Features</a> — long essays on the fox spirit, the underworld, the bestiary, the swordsmen.</li>
          <li>The whole <a href="/stories">Story Library</a> — filter by creature, book or dynasty.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          And if you want one strange tale in your inbox every week, there is the
          <a href="/about">newsletter</a>. Welcome to the library. The weird has been waiting for you for
          two thousand years; it is patient.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
